var e={domain:`frontend`,slug:`react-native`,title:`React Native & Expo`,order:13,summary:`How React skills transfer to mobile, and what's different.`,questions:[{id:`rn-vs-web`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How does React Native differ from React on the web?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`react-native`,`mobile`],explain:"Same React model (components, hooks, state), but you render **native** views instead of DOM elements: `<View>`, `<Text>`, `<Image>`, `<Pressable>` instead of `div`/`span`/`button`. Styling is a JavaScript subset of CSS with Flexbox by default (column direction), there's no CSS cascade, and all text must sit inside `<Text>`.",hook:`🔁 Same brain (React), different body (native views instead of the DOM).`,example:"```tsx\n<View style={{ padding: 16, gap: 8 }}>\n  <Text style={{ fontSize: 18, fontWeight: '600' }}>Hello</Text>\n  <Pressable onPress={save}><Text>Save</Text></Pressable>\n</View>\n```",html:{explain:`<p>Same React model (components, hooks, state), but you render <strong>native</strong> views instead of DOM elements: <code>&#x3C;View></code>, <code>&#x3C;Text></code>, <code>&#x3C;Image></code>, <code>&#x3C;Pressable></code> instead of <code>div</code>/<code>span</code>/<code>button</code>. Styling is a JavaScript subset of CSS with Flexbox by default (column direction), there's no CSS cascade, and all text must sit inside <code>&#x3C;Text></code>.</p>`,hook:`<p>🔁 Same brain (React), different body (native views instead of the DOM).</p>`,example:`<div class="codeblock"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-tsx hljs">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-title class_">View</span> style={{ <span class="hljs-attr">padding</span>: <span class="hljs-number">16</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">gap</span>: <span class="hljs-number">8</span> }}>
  <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Text</span> <span class="hljs-attr">style</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{{</span> <span class="hljs-attr">fontSize:</span> <span class="hljs-attr">18</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">fontWeight:</span> '<span class="hljs-attr">600</span>' }}></span>Hello<span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>></span></span>
  <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Pressable</span> <span class="hljs-attr">onPress</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{save}</span>></span><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>></span>Save<span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>></span><span class="hljs-tag">&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-name">Pressable</span>></span></span>
&#x3C;/<span class="hljs-title class_">View</span>>
</code></pre><button type="button" class="copy-btn" aria-label="Copy code">Copy</button></div>`}},{id:`rn-expo`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`What is Expo, and when would you not use it?`,difficulty:`easy`,tags:[`expo`,`tooling`],explain:`Expo is a framework and toolchain around React Native: file-based routing (Expo Router), a large set of native modules, over-the-air updates, and cloud builds (EAS). With development builds and config plugins you can add custom native code too, so the old "eject" step is rarely needed. Skip it only for unusual native requirements or when an existing native app already dictates the setup.`,hook:`🧰 Expo is React Native with batteries, a charger and a delivery service included.`,html:{explain:`<p>Expo is a framework and toolchain around React Native: file-based routing (Expo Router), a large set of native modules, over-the-air updates, and cloud builds (EAS). With development builds and config plugins you can add custom native code too, so the old "eject" step is rarely needed. Skip it only for unusual native requirements or when an existing native app already dictates the setup.</p>`,hook:`<p>🧰 Expo is React Native with batteries, a charger and a delivery service included.</p>`}},{id:`rn-architecture`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How does React Native's architecture work?`,difficulty:`hard`,tags:[`react-native`,`architecture`],explain:`Your JavaScript runs in a JS engine (Hermes) and drives native UI on the platform's main thread. The **New Architecture** replaces the old asynchronous JSON "bridge" with **JSI** (direct JS↔native calls), **TurboModules** (lazily loaded native modules) and **Fabric** (a new renderer supporting synchronous layout and concurrent React).`,hook:`🌉 The old bridge was a ferry carrying JSON letters; JSI is a direct road between JS and native.`,html:{explain:`<p>Your JavaScript runs in a JS engine (Hermes) and drives native UI on the platform's main thread. The <strong>New Architecture</strong> replaces the old asynchronous JSON "bridge" with <strong>JSI</strong> (direct JS↔native calls), <strong>TurboModules</strong> (lazily loaded native modules) and <strong>Fabric</strong> (a new renderer supporting synchronous layout and concurrent React).</p>`,hook:`<p>🌉 The old bridge was a ferry carrying JSON letters; JSI is a direct road between JS and native.</p>`}},{id:`rn-navigation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you handle navigation in React Native?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`navigation`,`expo-router`],explain:"Use React Navigation (stack, tab and drawer navigators) or Expo Router, which builds on it with file-based routes like Next.js. Screens stay mounted in a stack, so think about focus events (`useFocusEffect`) for refreshing data when a user comes back to a screen.",hook:`🗂️ A navigation stack is a pile of cards: push to go deeper, pop to come back, and the cards underneath are still there.`,html:{explain:`<p>Use React Navigation (stack, tab and drawer navigators) or Expo Router, which builds on it with file-based routes like Next.js. Screens stay mounted in a stack, so think about focus events (<code>useFocusEffect</code>) for refreshing data when a user comes back to a screen.</p>`,hook:`<p>🗂️ A navigation stack is a pile of cards: push to go deeper, pop to come back, and the cards underneath are still there.</p>`}},{id:`rn-lists`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you make long lists fast in React Native?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`performance`,`lists`],explain:"Never render big arrays with `map` inside a `ScrollView`; use `FlatList` or Shopify's `FlashList`, which virtualise rows. Give items stable `keyExtractor` keys, memoise row components, keep row renders cheap, and set `getItemLayout` when rows have fixed heights.",hook:`🪟 Only build the rows that are on screen, and recycle them as they scroll away.`,html:{explain:`<p>Never render big arrays with <code>map</code> inside a <code>ScrollView</code>; use <code>FlatList</code> or Shopify's <code>FlashList</code>, which virtualise rows. Give items stable <code>keyExtractor</code> keys, memoise row components, keep row renders cheap, and set <code>getItemLayout</code> when rows have fixed heights.</p>`,hook:`<p>🪟 Only build the rows that are on screen, and recycle them as they scroll away.</p>`}},{id:`rn-storage`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you store data and handle secrets on mobile?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`storage`,`security`],explain:"`AsyncStorage` (or the faster MMKV) for non-sensitive key-value data, SQLite for structured offline data, and the platform keychain/keystore via `expo-secure-store` for tokens. Never ship real secrets in the app bundle: anything in the binary can be extracted, so call a backend that holds the secret.",hook:`🔐 The app bundle is a glass box: anything you put inside can be read.`,html:{explain:`<p><code>AsyncStorage</code> (or the faster MMKV) for non-sensitive key-value data, SQLite for structured offline data, and the platform keychain/keystore via <code>expo-secure-store</code> for tokens. Never ship real secrets in the app bundle: anything in the binary can be extracted, so call a backend that holds the secret.</p>`,hook:`<p>🔐 The app bundle is a glass box: anything you put inside can be read.</p>`}},{id:`rn-testing-release`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you test and release a React Native app?`,difficulty:`medium`,tags:[`testing`,`release`],explain:`Unit and component tests with Jest and React Native Testing Library, and end-to-end tests with Detox or Maestro. Build and submit with EAS (or Fastlane), and ship JavaScript-only fixes with over-the-air updates. Store review still applies to native changes.`,hook:`🚀 Native changes go through the app store; JavaScript fixes can fly over the air.`,html:{explain:`<p>Unit and component tests with Jest and React Native Testing Library, and end-to-end tests with Detox or Maestro. Build and submit with EAS (or Fastlane), and ship JavaScript-only fixes with over-the-air updates. Store review still applies to native changes.</p>`,hook:`<p>🚀 Native changes go through the app store; JavaScript fixes can fly over the air.</p>`}}]};export{e as default};